#!/bin/sh
# script/boot-check: boot every architecture's default kernel and watch it climb the boot ladder.
#
#   script/boot-check                     all three architectures, verdict green and a prompt reached
#   script/boot-check --arch riscv64      one leg
#   script/boot-check --inject            all three, with a failure injected: the verdict must be RED
#
# **This is the gate that makes milestone 268's boot ladder mean something.** The kernel describes
# the machine, self-tests, and hands over; the self-test *reports* rather than gating, because a
# machine you cannot log into is a machine you cannot fix (calef, 2026-09-09). Something else
# therefore has to read the verdict, or the milestone ships the defect it was written to fix: the
# boot tour's checks have printed `FAILED:` and carried on since they were written, and nothing ever
# read those either, which is why a stage that could never fire on two of three architectures hid
# for months.
#
# What it asserts, per architecture:
#
#   - the default kernel boots at all, in the configuration `cargo xtask run` gives a person
#   - the machine description printed to its end (`nife machine: ...` is its LAST line)
#   - the self-test verdict is `nife self-test: N of N passed` with nothing failed
#   - the boot handed over and `swish` came up, which is the ladder's top rung and the stated
#     terminal state of a default boot
#
# It reads those with `crates/serial_console`, the same recogniser `cargo xtask board-console` points
# at a real board over a serial cable. One reader, so a gate cannot pass something a bench run would
# call broken.
#
# `--inject` builds each kernel with `--features self_test_injection`, which makes one check report
# failure, and requires the run to come back RED. **A gate that has only ever been seen green is a
# gate nobody has tested**; this is milestone 268's proof condition, and it is cheap enough to keep.
#
# The archive, and no disk. A prompt cannot be reached without the archive: the kernel hands the
# machine to the progenitor, which loads the console, the line discipline, the input driver and
# `swish` out of it by name. Nothing here types, so nothing here needs a filesystem.
#
# **It boots the PVH `-kernel` image on x86_64, not the UEFI one.** `script/swish-check`'s x86_64
# leg boots `BOOTX64.EFI` under OVMF and pays about six minutes for it, because under firmware the
# console server waits for every byte to be painted on the screen. That is milestone 400 (the shell
# on the firmware's screen), and its fidelity buys the typing gate the loader, the firmware memory
# map and the screen tee; it buys this one nothing it asserts, because the prompt arrives on COM1
# either way and this is a gate a person runs before a push.
#
# BUGS
#
# **The prompt rung is the shell's banner, not the `$ `.** Two bytes is too weak to key on in a log
# that has just carried a kilobyte of hex (`crates/boot_ladder`'s own BUGS). So this proves `swish`
# started and printed, not that a prompt was offered or that anything could be typed at it;
# `script/swish-check` makes the stronger claim by typing, and this does not duplicate it.
#
# **Its kernels are debug builds, and only x86_64 has a release boot beside it.** CI's
# `boot-check` job also runs `script/ci-build release-uefi-boot`, the release x86_64 UEFI image
# under OVMF to the shell, because a defect the optimizer exposes (an `asm!` operand sharing a live
# input's register) is invisible at debug. Nothing boots a release aarch64 or riscv64 kernel, so the
# same class on those two architectures reaches a bench before any check sees it.
#
# **A riscv64 boot that wedges between its tour and its prompt takes the full 180-second cap to
# report**, rather than the 30-second quiet window, because that window is suppressed from the
# `Stage::Tour` rung up and riscv64 prints its tour BELOW the prompt. The suppression is right for
# its own reason (a boot that halts after the tour is a good boot, not a quiet one) and the cost
# lands here.
#
# **A red verdict ends each leg's watch early**, so a kernel whose self-test fails and then panics
# is reported as the self-test failure. That is the right first thing to report; the log named in
# the output has the rest.
#
# **Three archives and three debug kernels under TCG is about 26 seconds** against a warm target
# directory (patagonia, 2026-09-19), up from 19 before the prompt rung was asserted. The boot itself
# costs nothing extra, under three seconds a leg either way; the six seconds are packing the
# archives, and two of the three packers are a no-op against a tree `script/test` has already built.
# Each leg is killed rather than waited on (a boot that reaches a prompt sits at it and never
# exits). Run it when you touch the boot path, the machine description, the self-tests, or what the
# hand-over grants userspace.
#
# Name: provisional (milestone 268 (every architecture boots the same way)). It sits beside `script/swish-check` and is named the same way,
# which is deliberate and also inherits that name's recorded problem: `script/lint` runs
# `shellcheck`, and this family of `-check` entry points is one hyphen from several unrelated
# things. calef has not ruled.

set -e
# A heavy job: wait for one of the machine's heavy-job slots first (helpers/heavy_slot.py).
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh"
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh
exec cargo xtask boot-check "$@"
